WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.775
Picture two cooks at one counter, sharing a single cutting board.

2
00:00:06.775 --> 00:00:07.832
One chops onions.

3
00:00:07.832 --> 00:00:09.324
The other chops peppers.

4
00:00:09.324 --> 00:00:11.873
They never touch each other's vegetables.

5
00:00:11.873 --> 00:00:18.340
But only one knife fits on the board at a time, so each cook keeps stepping back, to wait for the other.

6
00:00:18.533 --> 00:00:21.410
Now, a question that feels too silly to ask.

7
00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:24.156
My two threads never touch the same field.

8
00:00:24.156 --> 00:00:25.790
No shared data, no locks.

9
00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:28.928
So how could they possibly slow each other down?

10
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.494
To answer that, we go one level down, into the processor.

11
00:00:34.494 --> 00:00:38.524
Main memory is far away, as distances inside a computer go.

12
00:00:38.524 --> 00:00:44.057
So each core, the part that runs one thread, keeps small, fast memories close by.

13
00:00:44.057 --> 00:00:45.560
They're called caches.

14
00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:48.404
And caches don't hold single variables.

15
00:00:48.404 --> 00:00:51.280
They hold cache lines: blocks of 64 bytes.

16
00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.772
The hardware moves the whole line, never one field.

17
00:00:54.772 --> 00:00:56.484
That's our cutting board.

18
00:00:56.484 --> 00:00:59.292
It moves as a unit, never a single onion.

19
00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:05.300
Each core keeps its own copy of the lines it uses, and the hardware keeps the copies in step.

20
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:09.315
But a core that wants to write a line needs the only valid copy.

21
00:01:09.315 --> 00:01:14.271
Every other copy is marked invalid, and must be fetched again before it's used.

22
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.114
Now the report service.

23
00:01:17.114 --> 00:01:20.443
A parser thread counts documents parsed.

24
00:01:20.443 --> 00:01:23.688
A writer thread counts reports written.

25
00:01:23.688 --> 00:01:27.017
Two volatile longs, in one small object.

26
00:01:27.017 --> 00:01:37.836
JOL, a small OpenJDK library, reads the real layout from the running JVM: parsed at offset 16, written at 24, in a 32 byte object.

27
00:01:37.836 --> 00:01:39.334
Eight bytes apart.

28
00:01:39.334 --> 00:01:40.249
Same board.

29
00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:42.125
So let's map it.

30
00:01:42.125 --> 00:01:43.985
The cooks are the two threads.

31
00:01:43.985 --> 00:01:46.216
Their vegetables are the two fields.

32
00:01:46.216 --> 00:01:47.952
The board is the cache line.

33
00:01:47.952 --> 00:01:51.919
And stepping back for the knife is a core, waiting for the line.

34
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.291
Let's trace two writes.

35
00:01:54.291 --> 00:01:57.273
Core 1 holds the line, and adds one to parsed.

36
00:01:57.273 --> 00:01:59.543
Core 2 wants to add one to written.

37
00:01:59.543 --> 00:02:03.497
Its copy is invalid, so it waits while the line travels over.

38
00:02:03.497 --> 00:02:08.684
Now core 1's copy is invalid, so its next write waits for the line to come back.

39
00:02:08.867 --> 00:02:12.827
Each increment is a few nanoseconds of work, wrapped in a wait.

40
00:02:12.827 --> 00:02:14.524
The program shares nothing.

41
00:02:14.524 --> 00:02:16.158
The hardware shares a lot.

42
00:02:16.158 --> 00:02:17.981
This is called false sharing.

43
00:02:18.867 --> 00:02:21.364
FalseSharingDemo runs exactly this.

44
00:02:21.364 --> 00:02:24.218
Two threads, 25 million increments each.

45
00:02:24.218 --> 00:02:29.855
First with the fields side by side, then padded apart, onto lines of their own.

46
00:02:29.855 --> 00:02:31.710
How much faster is padded?

47
00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:32.852
Pause and guess.

48
00:02:35.867 --> 00:02:43.495
On a quiet day, on the course machine, adjacent took 957 to 1,387 milliseconds.

49
00:02:43.495 --> 00:02:45.716
Padded took 206 to 247.

50
00:02:45.716 --> 00:02:52.185
About five times faster, from nothing but space between two fields.

51
00:02:52.333 --> 00:02:59.600
But here's the same demo, same machine, on a busy day: 994 against 785.

52
00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:01.238
977 against 784.

53
00:03:01.238 --> 00:03:02.466
A small gap.

54
00:03:02.466 --> 00:03:07.891
And across nine runs, padded lost one round in three.

55
00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:10.281
So was the story wrong?

56
00:03:10.281 --> 00:03:10.474
No.

57
00:03:10.474 --> 00:03:17.814
More than twenty busy programs shared those four cores, so our two threads were rarely running at the same moment.

58
00:03:17.814 --> 00:03:22.063
A line only bounces between two cores that both want it right now.

59
00:03:22.063 --> 00:03:25.089
Our cooks were mostly working different shifts.

60
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.172
And notice what never changed.

61
00:03:28.172 --> 00:03:31.069
Every count ended at exactly 25 million.

62
00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:34.255
False sharing costs time, never correctness.

63
00:03:35.133 --> 00:03:37.911
Here's where the cutting board picture breaks.

64
00:03:37.911 --> 00:03:41.594
A cook who waits for the board notices, and it costs seconds.

65
00:03:41.594 --> 00:03:47.753
A core waits nanoseconds for a line, and nobody notices, until it happens a few hundred million times.

66
00:03:47.753 --> 00:03:50.349
That's why you measure instead of guessing.

67
00:03:51.267 --> 00:03:52.487
The cure is space.

68
00:03:52.487 --> 00:03:59.536
Keep at least 64 bytes between fields that different threads write: give each cook a board of their own.

69
00:03:59.536 --> 00:04:06.518
The dependable way in Java is a chain of classes, because superclass fields are usually laid out first.

70
00:04:06.518 --> 00:04:12.755
With eight padding longs between them, JOL puts written at offset 88, 72 bytes after parsed.

71
00:04:13.667 --> 00:04:16.336
Here's the chain in Java and in Kotlin.

72
00:04:16.336 --> 00:04:20.922
Each hot field, and each block of padding, gets a class of its own.

73
00:04:20.922 --> 00:04:27.425
In Kotlin the classes must be abstract or open, and JvmField makes each property a plain field.

74
00:04:28.333 --> 00:04:29.668
Now the guardrails.

75
00:04:29.668 --> 00:04:34.092
Padding costs memory: 160 bytes instead of 32, on every object.

76
00:04:34.092 --> 00:04:37.042
Fine for one counter, a lot for a million.

77
00:04:37.042 --> 00:04:41.397
So pad only fields that different threads write in a hot loop.

78
00:04:41.397 --> 00:04:43.433
And don't promise a speed-up.

79
00:04:43.433 --> 00:04:45.189
The mechanism is certain.

80
00:04:45.189 --> 00:04:46.243
The size isn't.

81
00:04:46.243 --> 00:04:50.808
Measure on the hardware, and under the load, you really run with.

82
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.265
Separate fields aren't separate memory.

83
00:04:53.265 --> 00:04:57.563
Fields that different threads write in a hot loop need lines of their own.

84
00:04:58.467 --> 00:05:02.988
The moral of the story: memory moves in 64 byte boards, not in fields.

85
00:05:02.988 --> 00:05:09.189
Two cooks who never touch each other's vegetables can still spend the day waiting for the board.

86
00:05:09.333 --> 00:05:10.123
Quick check.

87
00:05:10.123 --> 00:05:16.638
An array of longs, one slot per worker thread, and each thread adds to its own slot in a busy loop.

88
00:05:16.638 --> 00:05:18.612
Can they slow each other down?

89
00:05:21.867 --> 00:05:22.156
Yes.

90
00:05:22.156 --> 00:05:24.543
Slots 8 bytes apart share a line.

91
00:05:24.543 --> 00:05:32.788
And for a counter that many threads bump, the JDK has already done the padding for you: LongAdder, coming up next.
